okay today I'm going to talk to you  about electromagnetic induction now all  of that electromagnetic induction  basically means is being able to  generate an alternating current using a  moving magnet so whenever we have a  magnet moving within a coil of wire so  I'll show you how this works now imagine  we have a coal power station so in a  core power station there's my burning  coals and those burning coals turn water  into steam and that steam turns this  turbine now whenever we have a turbine  turning in order to generate electricity  so that could be coal nuclear  hydroelectric wind power whenever we  have a turbine this is the process  that's going on that turbine is  connected to this part here which is a  generator this generates the electricity  and in order to do that what we need is  a magnet moving within a coil of wire  now this magnet would actually be really  kind of inside this coil of wire but  I've just drawn it like this so I can  see what's going on so what happens is  as the turbine turns it also causes this  magnet here to rotate as well so this  magnet will be turning round and round  and round now whenever we have the  movement of a magnet within a coil of  wire we create an alternating current an  alternating current so if I were to just  draw a graph to show this type of  current it looks like this now the  reason why we generates an alternating  current or AC is because the poles of  this magnet are constantly switching  around north south north south as it  goes so as a result the electrons keep  on flowing in different directions we  establish an alternating current  now there's three things which can  affect the size of the current that we  induce one of them is the number of  turns so if we have loads and loads and  loads of turns of wire on this  get a coil here so loads and loads and  loads and loads of wire wrap to wrap  around round round around we will make  more current we will induce a bigger  current the second thing is a speed of  movement so again if our magnet is being  is moving at a higher speed is rotating  at higher speed we will create again  induce a bigger current and the final  one is the strength of the magnet so the  stronger the magnet is the stronger the  magnet is the bigger the current that  we're going to induce so that  essentially is electromagnetic induction  being able to utilize the movement of a  magnet within a coil of wire in order to  produce an alternating current just as a  side point I just want to show you the  difference between alternating current  and direct current a good example of  something which produces direct current  is a battery because in a battery the  electrons only flow in a single  direction the graph of direct current  looks quite different so there's my axis  direct current is just like that because  the electrons just flow in one direction  so just remember in electromagnetic  induction whenever we use a turbine it  produces alternating currents not direct  current because the poles of those  magnets are constantly constantly  switching round so as a result the  electrons are constantly flowing in  different directions this is how  electricity is generated for any type of  power plant that has a turbine as part  of its mechanism this part right here  with the magnet and the coil of wire  this is what is inside a generator thank  you
 

